everyone loves a good mystery and now  seems as good a time as ever to dive  into the mystery movie genre luckily  there are plenty of films out there just  begging to be discovered from perplexing  puzzles to harrowing whodunits here are  the best mysterious movies currently on  Netflix if there's one singular mystery  that's puzzled and terrified humankind  since the dawn of time it's this what  happens to us when we die unsurprisingly  this is a topic that cinema has  approached in all kinds of ways but few  of those movies have been brave enough  to tackle the mysteries of the afterlife  as directly as Charlie McDowell's the  discovery the movie is set in a not too  distant future where a scientist named  Thomas Harbor has proven conclusively  that the afterlife does indeed exist  unfortunately he doesn't know exactly  where or what it really is in the wake  of mass suicides by folks  eager to find out for themselves those  answers become more vital than ever  years after the discovery harbors deeply  skeptical son and an enigmatic young  woman arrived at the scientists research  compound and find the search for answers  has led to the invention of a device  designed to record the afterlife  experience from there the discovery only  gets weirder in the best possible way  the film uses its central mystery to  frame a beautifully textured and  grounded character studies steeped in  simmering small-scale dramas grand  theological commentary and a dash of  heart-wrenching romance it also happens  to be a first-rate slice of hard sci-fi  cinema adapted from a novel by Koji  Suzuki hideo nakata near-flawless j  horror the ring was so successful that  inevitable english-language remake soon  followed in 2002  gore verbinski brought his version of  the ring to theaters and in doing so  unleashed one of the greatest horror  movies of the modern era  these days the rings plot is practically  iconic just in case you're still in the  dark however the movie follows a  reporter who stumbles onto a perplexing  story about a videotape that kills  anyone who watches it seven days later  want to know more then go watch it  because this isn't a movie you want  spoiled for you here's one guarantee  though you'll never see the film's  shocking final moments coming and  there's a good chance you'll be haunted  by them for years to come for many films  the mystery is the whole point of the  story sometimes however it's merely a  tool used to set in motion a far more  ambitious narrative in the case of Trey  Edward Schultz tramatic thriller it  comes at night the movie's horrifying  mystery serves as the entry point for  several more small-scale dramas driven  by fear paranoia and a desperate need to  protect the things that matter most the  story unfolds in the aftermath of the  pandemic event that has wiped out untold  numbers of the world's population  more specifically the film follows a man  and his family riding things out in a  secluded cabin in the woods only to find  their rigorously structured life upended  by the arrival of another family as is  the case with many mystery movies you  probably don't want to know much more  than it's really best to enter it comes  at night with as little information as  possible just know that as the film's  minor mysteries begin to unravel a far  more grueling picture of humanity  unfolds within them those mysteries are  made all the more affecting thanks to  the steely low-key direction of  burgeoning auteur Schultz not to mention  a series of fantastic turns from the  cast including Christopher Abbott Riley  Keough Carmen ejogo and Joel Edgerton if  creepy mystery movies have taught us  anything over the years it's that people  should probably be wary of long lost  friends turning up at their door clearly  someone forgot to tell that to Jason  Bateman's character in the gift because  when Simon and his wife Robin bump into  his old high school chum Gordo they  immediately strike up an awkward kinship  with the man the plot thickens when  Gordo begins making uninvited  appearances in Simon and Robins lives  and starts offering them some  curiously head-scratching gifts with  each new encounter tensions between the  three begin to ratchet up all the more  and it's not immediately clear why fear  not however as all is eventually  revealed in the gift and it soon becomes  obvious that the murky past of one of  the film's characters is coming back to  haunt them beyond that you'll just have  to find out for yourself but this  devious revenge movie expertly directed  by star Joel Edgerton cuts deep into the  psyches of its lead characters and is  bound to leave you pondering the pivotal  mystery long after the credits roll one  of the key ingredients of any great  mystery story is the fear of the unknown  and the hugely unnerving small-scale  sci-fi drama freaks is positively  steeped in it in fact it's that  particular fear that drives not just the  film's overarching story but every  brazen twist and turn contained within  its narrative the story follows one  brave little girl who's been kept in  total seclusion from the world by her  increasingly paranoid father in the  aftermath of what's presumed to be some  sort of apocalyptic event as the story  unfolds however it becomes clear that  the world outside isn't exactly what the  father has made of it but that doesn't  mean that he's totally lying either  freaks is a puzzle box of a film the  pieces of which eventually form a  twisted picture that'll likely extend  far beyond your expectations as such  part of the fun of the film is watching  the brilliant ways in which the movie  carefully places each piece allowing the  mysteries of freaks to reveal themselves  at a pace that simultaneously slow  burning and brashley kinetic in a  perfect world the name Asgar ferati  would already carry the same sort of  weight and reverence as that of David  Fincher or Darren Aronofsky though his  name is highly regarded in the realm of  international cinema  ferrati hasn't found the same notoriety  with North American audiences just yet  with any luck however the directors  subtle it savagely tense thriller  everybody knows will change that  although everybody knows is a  spanish-language film  don't let the language barrier put you  off because this is very much a classic  kind of thriller 1sd  in long buried secrets and mysteries as  it is in true blooded pulse-pounding  thrills for much of the film ferrati  takes the slow burn approach using the  first 30 minutes to build an intricately  constructed house of cards around the  protagonists inter familial  relationships but these characters  worlds begin to crumble when a beloved  family member goes missing in the wake  of the disappearance deep dark secrets  begin to find the light and everybody  knows pivotal mystery begins to unfurl  with Shakespearean weight of course it  also helps that along the way Penelope  Cruz and Javier Bardem deliver two of  their finest performances to date and  that's saying something nothing suits a  mystery quite like a cult this has a lot  to do with the fact that the cult  mentality is as puzzling as the  enigmatic figures who front the cults  themselves though Justin Benson and  Aaron Moorhead's 2017 Lovecraftian  stunner the endless takes the enigmatic  leader mostly out of the equation their  film remains a no less sinister  psychological study of cults themselves  one that also happens to be driven by a  horrifying supernatural mystery and more  appealing trying to explain an  impossible color the endless follows a  pair of brothers who years after  breaking free from the alien death cult  with which they spent much of their  youth are called back into the fold by a  mysterious force as bizarre as it is the  endless is the sort of film that only  works if you enter its narrative with a  wholly blank slate  it also masterfully blends elements of  fantasy sci-fi and horror all without  relying on a high budget or a list  actors of course that didn't stop the  endless from attracting near universal  critical acclaim on its release go in  blind come out terrified by now you  should be well-acquainted with the name  jeremy song yeh if not there's no better  place to start than with the astonishing  one-two throat punch delivered by his  2013 and 2015 thrillers blue ruin and  green room once you've gotten caught up  on sanyas wildly original beyond tense  cinematic style stick around netflix for  a little longer and  treat yourself to the directors latest  beastly brooder hold the dark even  though it lacks some of the visceral  energy of his prior works hold the dark  is as accomplished a mystery film as any  other you'll find streaming set largely  among the gorgeous but unforgiving  vistas of the Alaskan wilderness hold  the dark follows famed Wolfe expert  Russell Corr who is called to an  isolated village after a six year old  boy is lost and presumed killed by a  pack of wolves once he arrives Kor finds  nothing is quite what it seems at the  town the case itself for the missing  boy's mother as Kor continues his  investigation saw me a twists hold the  dark from a basic missing person mystery  into a full-on heart of darkness style  metaphysical Odyssey complete with a  finale that will leave you baffled  breathless and broken in equal measure  if you're looking for a good  old-fashioned mystery movie that'll  tease your brain and raise your heart  rate at the same time you can always  look to the tried and true spy thriller  genre after all what could be better  than a story that's entirely built  around secrets in misdirection and if  its cold war intrigue year after then  look no further than Tomas Alfredson  immaculately executed adaptation of John  the car a spy novel Tinker Tailor  Soldier Spy if you're wondering why  alfred syns name might sound familiar  it's because prior to Tinker Tailor  Soldier Spy he previously directed  2008's pitch perfect vampire flick Let  The Right One In naturally Tinker Tailor  Soldier Spy and folds with the same  meticulous pacing and propulsive  menacing energy only this go-round  Alfredsson uses it to fuel a pitch-black  twist a minute thriller about the hunt  for a Russian agent embedded in mi6  leading the cast is an ever better Gary  Oldman who's surrounded by a veritable  who's who a british screen talent  including Tom Hardy Benedict Cumberbatch  Toby Jones John Hurt and Colin Firth  frankly if the thought of that  star-studded cast running down a spy  ring in the 1970s doesn't get you pumped  the mystery genre might not be for you  just saying as far as cinematic  subgenres Gote daytime horror continues  to be the one which most frequently  disturbs even the staunchest of genre  shinato's after all as unsettling as  horror movies can get when things go  bump in the night seeing those things  bump around in the light of day is often  twice as disturbing and although a few  notable additions have been made to the  daytime horror sub-genre of late the  gold standard remains the wicker man  no not that one this is Robin Hardy's  masterful 1973 thriller and as it  happens parties film is also a  near-flawless mystery movie whose unholy  secrets aren't revealed until it's  jaw-dropping final moments if you  haven't seen Hardy's original wicker man  for yourself the film follows a devout  detective who's dispatched to an  isolated Scottish island to track down a  missing girl when he arrives however the  townsfolk are quick to claim the girl  isn't missing because she never existed  in the first place from that central  mystery party weaves an intricately spun  web of intrigue that finds the detective  unknowingly playing fly to a  bloodthirsty spider to say more would be  to give the film away but suffice to say  that the film's portrayal of pagan  theology is downright terrifying and the  film's ending is the stuff of cinematic  legend check out one of our newest  videos right her